现在，对于我来说一切都不同了。
Now, of course, things are different for me.

35
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:07,330
我成为了电影演员，
I'm in the movies.

36
00:02:07,330 --> 00:02:09,610
已经不再为缺钱而担忧了。
Money isn't much of a worry.

37
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:13,720
我的经历表明出身贫寒
My experience shows that being born poor

38
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:17,340
并不能代表一生的贫困。
is not necessarily a life sentence.

39
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:20,890
但世界上的几十亿人里，
But for billions of people around the world,

40
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:25,180
一代代人的贫穷似乎永无止境。
poverty passes from generation to generation without end.

41
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:29,470
我们从父母那里继承财产，也可能没有。
We inherit property, or the lack of it, from our parents.

42
00:02:29,470 --> 00:02:32,140
我们也继承了他们的DNA，
We also inherit our parents' DNA,

43
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:35,640
我们现在知道这关乎了遗传学，
and for as long as we've known about genetics,

44
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:38,660
科学家想要知道在财富
scientists have been wondering if there is a connection

45
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:41,500
与基因之间是否存在联系。
between our money and our genes.

46
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:47,150
基因是告诉你的身体如何运作的一小段编码，
Genes are pieces of code that tell your body how to function.

47
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:51,290
人类DNA中大约有25000个基因，
There are about 25,000 genes in human DNA.

48
00:02:51,290 --> 00:02:55,210
它们如何影响到我们的个性？
How do they affect our personalities?

49
00:02:55,210 --> 00:02:56,760
这些问题，
These are questions

50
00:02:56,760 --> 00:03:00,350
弗吉尼亚大学的Eric Turkheimer教授
University of Virginia Professor Eric Turkheimer

51
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:02,470
已经探究了20年了。
has been asking for 20 years.

52
00:03:02,470 --> 00:03:08,510
Eric在复杂人类行为的遗传学方面研究很多。
Eric studies the genetics of complex human behavior.

53
00:03:08,510 --> 00:03:10,890
你可以把
One way you can think about the role

54
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:12,530
单个基因
that individual genes play

55
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:15,310
在复杂遗传效应中的作用
in the creation of complex genetic effects

56
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:18,620
看作是复杂织物中
is that it's like the role that an individual thread

57
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:22,540
一根线的作用。
might play in the creation of a complex tapestry.

58
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:25,460
你无法与其它线分离，
You couldn't really understand the role of each thread

59
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:27,660
单独理解一根线的含义，
one at a time, separate from the others.

60
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:30,380
你要知道每根线是如何
You have to know how each thread fits into the warp

61
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:31,750
编织成织物，
and woof of the fabric

62
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:34,470
它们如何在一起来形成花纹图案的。
and how they all work together to create the pattern.

63
00:03:34,470 --> 00:03:38,370
对于基因来说，道理是一样。
Well, with genes, it's the same way.

64
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:44,060
这些遗传上的相互作用
These are the genetic interactions

65
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:48,010
代表的是你体内23对染色体之间的关系。
within just one of your 23 pairs of chromosomes.

66
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:51,320
遗传因子高度交联，形成了
It's a highly interconnected, highly complex web

67
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:53,650
高度复杂的网络。
of genetic factors.

68
00:03:53,650 --> 00:03:58,690
人类基因组测序于2001年完成后，
After the human genome was decoded in 2001,

69
00:03:58,690 --> 00:04:03,730
很多研究声称发现了一些单个基因
studies emerged claiming to find links between individual genes

70
00:04:03,730 --> 00:04:06,950
与比如常识、野心和坚韧
and characteristics like common sense,

71
00:04:06,950 --> 00:04:10,590
这些性格之间的联系。
ambition, and perseverance.

72
00:04:10,590 --> 00:04:15,210
有些人甚至认为我们很快就能分辨出
Some imagined we might soon be able to identify who was born

73
00:04:15,210 --> 00:04:18,260
谁注定成功或失败。
to prosper and who was born to fail.

74
00:04:18,260 --> 00:04:21,100
这种观点是很可怕的，
This has frightening implications.

75
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:26,350
想象下银行放贷时需要检测你的唾液、
Imagine banks demanding saliva tests before granting loans,

76
00:04:26,350 --> 00:04:29,600
具有更能赚钱的基因的父亲或母亲
custody battles decided by which parent

77
00:04:29,610 --> 00:04:32,560
才能获得抚养权、
has better financial genes,

78
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:36,310
或是某个民族都带上了信用危机的标签。
or entire races labeled as credit risks.

79
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:38,030
但随后几十年，
But a decade later,

80
00:04:38,030 --> 00:04:42,530
这些对于一个基因一种性格的大量研究
the vast majority of these one-gene, one-trait studies

81
00:04:42,540 --> 00:04:44,540
被证明是错误的。
have been proven wrong.

82
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:47,460
如果你20年前问我，
I think if you would ask me 20 years ago,

83
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:51,790
“我们在20年内能够找到
"are we gonna know what the genes for human personality

84
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:53,300
与性格相关的基因么？”
are in 20 years?"

85
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:54,580
我可能会说，
I think I would've said,

86
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:56,470
“是的，当然，我们会找到一些基因的。”
"Yeah, sure, we'll know something."

87
00:04:56,470 --> 00:05:00,050
但现在我们知道基因的工作方式
It's turning out that the way genetics works

88
00:05:00,050 --> 00:05:03,120
更为复杂，我们甚至
is more complicated in a way that doesn't allow us

89
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:05,920
不能鉴定单个基因的作用。
to identify the effects of individual genes.

90
00:05:05,930 --> 00:05:09,630
于是Eric使用了一种新方法：
So Eric has a new approach:

91
00:05:09,630 --> 00:05:13,970
通过软件分析世界各地的双胞胎
Using statistical software to analyze the vast body of data

92
00:05:13,970 --> 00:05:17,490
从他们那里得到的大量数据。
collected around the world on twins.

93
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:23,110
你是由基因还是环境决定的呢？
Are you shaped by your genes or by your environment?

94
00:05:23,110 --> 00:05:26,580
这是一个总会遇到的问题，关于如何将基因
It's the problem that always gets in the way of studies

95
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:31,030
与复杂特征联系起来的问题。
on how genes are connected to complex traits.

96
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:32,950
双胞胎则提供了一种可能，
But twins offer a way

97
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:37,040
能够将先天与后天作用分离。
to separate the effects of nature and nurture.

98
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:42,340
同卵双胞胎的遗传相似性近乎100%，
Identical twins share virtually 100% of their genetic material.

99
00:05:42,350 --> 00:05:47,500
异卵双胞胎也有50%的基因相似性，
Fraternal twins share 50% of their genes.

100
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:49,940
通过比较同卵和异卵双胞胎，
By comparing identical and fraternal twins,

101
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:53,390
我们能够大体证明
we were able to demonstrate in a general way

102
00:05:53,390 --> 00:05:59,180
基因对人的所有方面都有影响。
that genes have effects on almost everything.

103
00:05:59,180 --> 00:06:02,400
Eric在全美国
Eric began looking at very large samples

104
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:05,020
和所有社会经济群体中
of twins from all around the U.S.

105
00:06:05,020 --> 00:06:07,190
搜集了大量的双胞胎数据，
From all socioeconomic groups.

106
00:06:07,190 --> 00:06:12,020
他使用IQ值预测一个孩子的未来财富，
He used I.Q. as a predictor of future wealth of a child,

107
00:06:12,030 --> 00:06:15,910
许多研究表明它们的关系非常密切。
a link most studies find to be very strong.

108
00:06:15,910 --> 00:06:18,550
我们发现的非常令人震惊，
What we found was quite surprising.

109
00:06:18,550 --> 00:06:21,380
穷养起来的孩子，
Children raised in poverty,

110
00:06:21,390 --> 00:06:25,200
他们的家庭环境是最重要的因素，
their home environment was by far the most important factor.

111
00:06:25,210 --> 00:06:28,370
而基因似乎并没有发挥作用。
Their genes seemed to play almost no role at all.

112
00:06:28,380 --> 00:06:32,640
如果一个孩子携带有
If a child with genes predisposing him or her

113
00:06:32,650 --> 00:06:34,760
可能成为金融奇才的基因，
to be a financial wizard

114
00:06:34,770 --> 00:06:38,720
却在极为恶劣的环境中成长起来，
is raised in an extremely harsh environment,

115
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:42,550
那这段DNA就无法使其成才。
that exceptional DNA may not shine through.

116
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:45,160
而把孩子的成长
And then as you moved from kids

117
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:47,860
从糟糕的环境中
raised in those really terrible circumstances

118
00:06:47,860 --> 00:06:51,110
转移到中产阶级家庭，
up to kids raised in the middle class,

119
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:54,620
基因的作用就变得越来越重要了，
the role of genes became more and more and more important,

120
00:06:54,620 --> 00:06:57,590
在中产阶级或更好的氛围中，
and once you got to the middle class or better,

121
00:06:57,590 --> 00:06:59,090
基因才是最重要的，
genes took over,

122
00:06:59,090 --> 00:07:02,210
研究中的最富有的家庭中，
and by the time you got to the wealthiest people in the study,

123
00:07:02,210 --> 00:07:04,880
基因完全起了决定性作用。
genes were making all the difference.

124
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:09,630
基因对于富人家的
Genes do have important effects

125
00:07:09,630 --> 00:07:13,250
和穷人家孩子都产生了重要影响，
on how rich or poor children will be when they grow up,

126
00:07:13,250 --> 00:07:16,100
但是这种作用只有在
but it's an effect that is only visible

127
00:07:16,110 --> 00:07:18,920
较为富裕的孩子身上有所体现。
when kids grow up outside of poverty.

128
00:07:20,360 --> 00:07:22,030
某种程度上来说，
To some extent,

129
00:07:22,030 --> 00:07:25,900
在贫穷环境中的人们
it's probably true that people who wind up living in poverty

130
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:28,430
与赚钱多的人之间
have some kind of genetic difference

131
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:31,450
是会存在一些遗传差异，
from people who wind up making a lot of money.

132
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:33,120
我认为我们的工作表明
I think what our work has shown

133
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:36,110
什么样的遗传倾向对于
is that it doesn't matter what kind of genetic tendencies

134
00:07:36,110 --> 00:07:37,740
孩子的成长可能没什么关系，
some of these kids may have had.

135
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:40,250
如果他们成长的环境很糟，
If they're raised in a bad enough environment,

136
00:07:40,250 --> 00:07:42,080
这些基因就不会表达。
they're not gonna be able to express them.

137
00:07:42,080 --> 00:07:44,500
基因的“纺织物”
The tapestry of genes

138
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:46,800
造就了已经成人的你，
that make up you as an adult

139
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:50,120
其中包括与生俱来的DNA
are shaped by the fabric of DNA you were born with

140
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:53,640
以及你成长的环境。
and by the environment you grew up in.

141
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:59,230
我们如何扭转在婴幼儿时
How can we overcome the cards that nature and nurture dealt us

142
00:07:59,230 --> 00:08:02,520
先天(本性)与后天(培养)为我们定好的命运呢？
in the womb and the crib?

143
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:06,140
一项新的研究表明我们未来的经济条件
A new study suggests our economic destiny

144
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:09,320
可能在12岁时就已经决定好了，
may be determined by the time we are 12,

145
00:08:09,330 --> 00:08:12,160
因为贫穷会影响大脑。
because poverty can affect the brain.

146
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:15,110
Martha Farah是宾夕法尼亚大学
Martha Farah is the founder and director

147
00:08:15,120 --> 00:08:18,280
神经科学与社会中心的
of the Center for Neuroscience and Society

148
00:08:18,290 --> 00:08:20,840
建立者与主管，
at the University of Pennsylvania.

149
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:23,820
她经常在想：
She has often wondered,

150
00:08:23,820 --> 00:08:26,790
“为什么穷人家的孩子在IQ测试
"Why do poor children perform worse on I.Q. tests

151
00:08:26,790 --> 00:08:28,680
和学校学习中的表现较差呢？”
and in school?"

152
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:31,000
许多不同的学科
Many different disciplines

153
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:35,550
都想要弄清楚贫穷
have tried to understand how it is that poverty

154
00:08:35,550 --> 00:08:38,390
如何塑造了人们的生活。
shapes people's life chances.

155
00:08:38,390 --> 00:08:42,390
我和我的同事正在尝试一种新的神经生物学方法
My colleagues and I are taking a neurobiological approach

156
00:08:42,390 --> 00:08:44,860
来解答这一问题。
to this question.

157
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:47,180
过去几年里，
Over the past few years,

158
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:51,480
Martha和她的同事扫描了上百个
Martha and her colleagues have scanned the brain architecture

159
00:08:51,490 --> 00:08:53,200
成长自
of hundreds of children

160
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:56,690
不同社会经济背景的儿童的脑部结构，
from a wide range of socioeconomic backgrounds.

161
00:08:56,690 --> 00:09:00,330
她发现富人家和穷人家的孩子
She has found there are physical differences

162
00:09:00,330 --> 00:09:04,050
大脑结构就存在不同。
between the brains of the rich and the poor.

163
00:09:04,050 --> 00:09:06,920
我们想要知道的问题是
The question we asked was,

164
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:10,140
“他们大脑的哪一部分
"What parts of the brain are dependent

165
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:12,040
“在不同经济条件下
"on that socioeconomic status

166
00:09:12,040 --> 00:09:16,730
会发育成如何的大小及形状？”
for the size and shape that they eventually grow to?"

167
00:09:16,730 --> 00:09:20,550
我们发现有一些重要区域的发育
What we found is that several important areas

168
00:09:20,550 --> 00:09:24,180
的确与经济条件状况有关。
do show an effect of socioeconomic status.

169
00:09:24,180 --> 00:09:27,020
在极度贫困中成长，
Growing up in extreme poverty

170
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:30,050
海马体则生长缓慢，
slows the growth of the hippocampus,

171
00:09:30,060 --> 00:09:34,890
海马体负责了学习、记忆和压力控制，
which is important for learning, memory, and stress regulation,

172
00:09:34,890 --> 00:09:38,280
同样前额叶皮层也会萎缩，
and it also shrinks the prefrontal cortex,

173
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:42,950
这块区域负责了协调记忆、感知和运动控制，
which helps coordinate memory, perception, and motor control.

174
00:09:42,950 --> 00:09:47,760
富裕人家孩子的大脑皮层更厚，
Rich kids tend to have a thicker cortex than poor kids,

